    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "body": "### Problem\n\nRunning `:h inclusive`, we can see the documentation on inclusive/exclusive motions:\n> Character motion is either inclusive or exclusive.  When inclusive, the\n> start and end position of the motion are included in the operation.  When\n> exclusive, the last character towards the end of the buffer is not included.\n> Linewise motions always include the start and end position.  Plugins can\n> check the v:event.inclusive flag of the |TextYankPost| event.\n\nBeing able to check `inclusive` would be very useful for custom operators. My personal usecase is making a \"place a cursor on this motion\" operator:\n```lua\nvim.keymap.set(\"n\", \"qd\", function()\n  local cursor = vim.api.nvim_win_get_cursor(0)\n  local was_after = false\n\n  vim.o.operatorfunc = function(type)\n    local mark = vim.api.nvim_buf_get_mark(0, \"[\")\n    if cursor[1] == mark[1] and cursor[2] == mark[2] then\n      mark = vim.api.nvim_buf_get_mark(0, \"]\")\n      was_after = true\n    end\n    if type ~= \"line\" and was_after then\n      mark[2] = mark[2] + 1\n    end\n\n    vim.api.nvim_buf_set_extmark(0, ns, mark[1] - 1, mark[2])\n    vim.api.nvim_win_set_cursor(0, cursor)\n  end\n\n  return \"1q=g@\"\nend, { expr = true })\n```\nAs written, this works as expected for most motions (`qdb`, `qdk`, `qdw`, `qdb`). However, any motion that's `inclusive` and searches after the cursor (`$`, `f`, `e`) will be placed incorrectly. For these to work properly, the condition for incrementing `mark[2]` needs an additional `and not inclusive`.\n\n### Expected behavior\n\nInformation on whether the previous motion was inclusive would be readable in contexts other than `TextYankPost`. My expectation would be a `vim.v` variable, but I'm not picky.",
